  "synthesis": "The three preprints share an evaluation-focused thread rather than a new architecture. The mammography study shows that AUC and C-index are insensitive to diagnosis timing and proposes a two-stage whether-versus-when model to address this. CALIFUSE treats disagreement among several frozen pathology foundation models as informative, using representational heterogeneity and Jensen-Shannon chunk agreement to obtain calibrated uncertainty in whole-slide image classification. The glioblastoma circulating microRNA study tests CTGAN and TVAE augmentation and finds that same-modality validation overstates performance, with cross-platform AUROC falling to roughly 0.40-0.47, near chance. Together these works question whether headline discrimination metrics reflect clinically relevant behavior, namely timing, calibration and transfer across platforms. All three are unreviewed preprints, so the findings remain provisional.",
